--- !u!114 &2018873943
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2018873941}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Sanchi Stupa is one of India\u2019s most important ancient Buddhist
    monuments and a remarkable example of early Buddhist architecture, sculpture,
    and symbolism. Located in Madhya Pradesh, the Great Stupa at Sanchi was originally
    commissioned by Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE. Over later centuries,
    the monument was enlarged and decorated, especially during the Shunga and Satavahana
    periods.\nThe main structure of the Stupa is the large hemispherical Anda, or
    dome. It represents the central form of the monument and traditionally contains
    sacred relics. Surrounding the dome is the Vedika, a stone railing that marks
    the sacred area. Visitors traditionally move around the Stupa in a clockwise
    direction as part of a practice known as circumambulation.\nAbove the dome is
    the Harmika, a small square enclosure. Rising above it is the Chhatra, an umbrella-like
    structure consisting of multiple circular elements. The Chhatra is associated
    with honour and spiritual significance.\nOne of the most distinctive features
    of Sanchi is its four beautifully decorated Toranas, or gateways. They face the
    four cardinal directions. Their carved panels contain scenes, symbols, animals,
    plants, and stories connected with Buddhist traditions. The artwork includes
    representations associated with the Jataka stories, which describe events connected
    with the Buddha\u2019s previous lives.\nThe Dharma Wheel is another important
    Buddhist symbol seen in the artistic decoration. At Sanchi, the Buddha is often
    represented through symbols rather than a direct human figure, including the
    wheel, Bodhi tree, footprints, and empty throne.\nThe Sanchi monuments form an
    important archaeological and cultural site and were designated a UNESCO World
    Heritage Site in 1989. Today, Sanchi provides an opportunity to understand ancient
    Indian architecture, Buddhist traditions, stone craftsmanship, and symbolic storytelling.\nFor
    the 3D experience, learners can rotate and zoom the complete model, explore the
    Anda, Vedika, Torana, Harmika, and Chhatra, and use an exploded view to understand
    how these architectural elements form the complete monument."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
